13-17 USED by/SHARED with the stokes add-on

18  viscoelastic fluid model

19  number of modes

20  if >0:
      start index in coefficient%r of the viscoelastic model material data.
      The value must be at least 501, which is where the free range begins.
    else if <0:
      (minus) array number in coefficients%ra2 containing the viscoelastic model
      material data. Thus, assuming m=-coefficients%i(20) then the 2D array
      coefficients%ra2(m)%a(1:np,1:nmodes), with np the number of parameters
      per mode, contains the model data.

21  log conformation transformation:
    0: no
    1: yes
    Note, that the log-conformation transformation and the btensor-formulation
    (coefficients%i(71)>=1) cannot be used at the same time.

22  Time integration scheme:
    1: first-order, semi-implicit Euler
    2: second-order, semi-implicit Crank-Nicolson/Adams-Bashforth
    3: first-order, explicit Euler
    4: second-order, explicit Adams-Bashforth
    5: second-order, semi-implicit Gear/Karniadakis
    6: second-order, semi-implicit Crank-Nicolson/conformation prediction
    7: second-order, semi-implicit Gear/conformation prediction
    8: first-order, implicit Euler (BDF1)
    9: second-order, implicit Crank-Nicolson (trapezoidal)
    10: second-order, implicit Gear (BDF2)
    11: first-order, theta method

23  USED by/SHARED with the stokes add-on

24  numerical integration rule for boundary integrals in the DG method
    for curves:
      n: number of Gauss-Legendre integration points. 1<=n<=5. For example
         with n=3 a three-point Gauss-Legendre integration is used.
    for surfaces with quadrilaterals:
      n: number of Gauss-Legendre integration points in _one_ coordinate
         direction. 1<=n<=5. For example with n=3 a 3x3 Gauss integration is
         used for quads

25  Type of implementation of the DG method:
    1: Explicit DG. The matrix and right-hand side of M dc/dt=... are build and
       it is up to the user to create a time integration scheme in the main
       program. coefficients%i(22) and coeffficients%r(8) (delta t) are not used
       and therefore have no effect.

26  mode1  Include mode1--mode2 in the viscoelastic model computations only.
27  mode2  Special case: coefficient%i(26)=0 means mode1=1
           Special case: coefficient%i(27)=0 means mode2=nmodes
    Thus specifying zeros means: all modes are computed.
    NOTE: overridden if coefficient%i(85)>0 if applicable.

28  component choice parameter in computing a derivative.
    For example coefficient%i(28) means the mode number in deriv_conformation
    and deriv_viscoelastic_stress_tensor.
    See the sources for the possibilities in various derivative routines.
    See also coefficients%i(13) in the stokes module.

**** the following two parameters (29,31) determine how h/U in the tau parameter
     of the SUPG method is computed: (see also coefficients%i(50))

29  htype, the type of h computation:
         0: take default value (=2)
         1: the standard velocity projection
         2: mapping of the reference element
         3: determined by the size of the element:
            2D: h = (elementsize)**0.5
            3D: h = (elementsize)**1/3
            hence: h is a constant per element

30  USED by/SHARED with the stokes add-on

31  Uscaling, how is the U scaling computed:
         0: take default value (=3)
         2: 1/N sum_i |u|_i , i=1,..,N with N the number of integration points.
         3: |u| in the integration points separately
         4: global scaling Uglobal = coefficient%r(10)

