89  Indicates whether the time-derivative term in the constitutive equation,
    and if applicable the evolution equation for gammap, will be included in
    the routine implicit_ce_supg_elem and implicit_gammap_supg_elem:
    0 yes
    1 No, the time-derivative term is excluded from the system.
      Excluding the time-derivative term will only build the Jacobian
      of the convection term and (minus) the right-hand side.
      Possible reasons for excluding the time-derivative term are:
      a. Perform Newton-Raphson iteration for the steady state, if it exists.
      b. Together with a separate built of the matrix of the time-derivative
         with the routine implicit_ce_timederiv_supg_elem, and if applicable
         the routine implicit_gammap_timederiv_supg_elem, we can assemble a
         generalized eigenvalue problem for linear stability analysis.

90  Apply rotation reinitialization to the b-tensor on element level:
    0 no
    1 Pointwise in each integration point. This is only available if
      the time integration scheme is such that the convection term needs to
      be evaluated at the new time step only.
    NOTE: A value >0 only applies if the contravariant deformation
    tensor (CDT) formulation tensor has been set (coefficients%i(71)=1)

91  Include Jacobian of the SUPG test function for the Newton-Raphson
    iteration scheme:
    0 no
    1 yes
    NOTE: This affects _only_ the following routines:
      implicit_ce_supg_elem
      steady_ce_timederiv_supg_elem
      implicit_gammap_supg_elem
      steady_gammap_timederiv_supg_elem
    and has no effect on other routines, such as
      implicit_ce_vel_supg_elem or
      implicit_ce_timederiv_supg_elem or
      implicit_gammap_timederiv_supg_elem.
    NOTE: By setting coefficients%i(91)=0 the routine implicit_ce_supg_elem
          can be used to iterate only on the conformation and keeping
          the velocity constant during iteration.

92  Indicates whether in the viscoelastic models that depend on the von Mises
    stress, the von Mises stress is defined per mode or using multiple modes:
    0: defined per mode
    1: using multiple modes. The modes are defined by the range mode1:mode2
       or coefficients%i(26):coefficients%i(27). The modes must be built in
       one call to build_system.

